.Dd October 7, 2026
.Dt CANCONFIG 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm canconfig
.Nd configure CAN network interface parameters
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl a
.Nm
.Ar canif
.Nm
.Ar can
.Ar command
.Op Ar args ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility is used to configure CAN network interface parameters and retrieve
CAN network interface parameters and status from the kernel.
Status includes the interface MTU, link-mode capabilities and enabled modes.
The
.Dv FD
capability indicates that the interface can accept CAN FD frames when that
mode is enabled.
.Ss Timing
The base time unit used to define the network bit rate is the time quanta (tq),
its value is defined by the input clock frequency and the prescaler value
as (1/ freq * prescaler).
.Pp
The network bit time is split in 4 segments:
.Bl -tag -width "phase_seg1" -offset indent -compact
.It sync_seg
fixed to 1 tq
.It prop_seg
.It phase_seg1
.It phase_seg2
.El
The network bit time is (1 + prop_seg + phase_seg1 + phase_seg2), in tq.
The sample point is between phase_seg1 and phase_seg2.
.Pp
The receiver hardware is allowed to shorten prop_seg or phase_seg2 to
resynchronize with the sender.
The swj (Synchronisation Jump Width) parameter
defines by how much the hardware can shorten these segments.
.Pp
Some hardware can use 3 sample points instead of one, and keep the majority as
the final value.
.Pp
Virtual interfaces with no input clock, such as
.Xr canloop 4 ,
have no physical bit timing.
Their status displays this explicitly, and timing commands are rejected.
The timing commands describe the existing nominal timing parameters;
they do not configure a separate CAN FD data-phase bit rate.
.Ss Options
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl a
Display the status of all CAN devices present on the system.
This flag is mutually exclusive with all other sub-commands.
.El
.Pp
All other operations require that a CAN interface be specified.
If a CAN interface is specified with no sub-commands,
the status of that interface is displayed.
.Pp
The following sub-commands are available:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Cm up
Start network operations on the interface.
Physical interfaces require that all timing parameters have been set.
.It Cm down
Stop network operations on the interface.
.It Cm brp Ar value
Set the value of the baud rate prescaler.
.It Cm prop_seg Ar value
Set the number of tq for the propagation segment.
.It Cm phase_seg1 Ar value
Set the number of tq for the phase segment 1.
.It Cm phase_seg2 Ar value
Set the number of tq for the phase segment 2.
.It Cm sjw Ar value
Set the number of tq for the Synchronisation Jump Width.
.It Cm 3samples
Enable triple-sampling.
.It Cm -3samples
Disable triple-sampling.
.It Cm listenonly
Enable listen-only mode.
In this mode the controller is passive, and doesn't send ACKs on the bus.
.It Cm -listenonly
Disable listen-only mode.
.It Cm loopback
Enable loopback mode.
In this mode, the controller doesn't expect ACK from the bus.
.It Cm -loopback
Disable loopback mode.
.It Cm fd
Enable CAN FD mode on an interface advertising the
.Dv FD
capability.
The interface must already be down.
For
.Xr canloop 4 ,
this changes the MTU from 16 to 72 bytes.
Applications must also enable the
.Dv CAN_RAW_FD_FRAMES
socket option described in
.Xr can 4 .
.It Cm -fd
Disable CAN FD mode while the interface is down.
For
.Xr canloop 4 ,
this restores the 16-byte classical CAN MTU.
.El
.Pp
The
.Cm up
and
.Cm down
commands apply interface flags at the end of an invocation.
Use a separate
.Cm down
invocation before changing CAN FD mode on an active interface;
combining
.Cm down fd
does not bring the interface down before the mode change.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Create and enable a virtual CAN FD interface:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ifconfig canlo0 create
canconfig canlo0 down
canconfig canlo0 fd
canconfig canlo0 up
canconfig canlo0
.Ed
Return it to classical CAN mode:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
canconfig canlo0 down
canconfig canlo0 -fd
canconfig canlo0 up
.Ed
These commands exercise a software interface and do not configure physical
CAN FD hardware or a data-phase bit rate.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr can 4 ,
.Xr canloop 4 ,
.Xr ifconfig.if 5 ,
.Xr ifconfig 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
utility first appeared in
.Nx 8.0 .
.Sh AUTHORS
This version of the
.Nm
utility was written by
.An Manuel Bouyer .
